    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAEn//4Xw\u003d",
        "filename": "etc/keystone.conf.ldap",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1916
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-02-21T21:43:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What about other schema? They should be configurable as well. Not all LDAP deployments are exactly the same. AD schema are very different from OpenLDAP or Novell. At the very least, we should make objectclasses, attribute to identify username (i.e. uid, cn, or samaccountname?), search scope (subtree or onelevel?), search filter configurable.\n\nFurthermore, SSL is a must for any LDAP authentication. No security auditor will let you get away with deploying this in a production environment knowing LDAP bind will be going over the clear.\n\nAlso, you may want to think about supporting local caching to boost performance. LDAP data are highly static, which are perfect candidates for caching.",
      "revId": "81456d38b3eaba484c9c52d7030db9c74ee46ab8",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
